lesson k-measuring-with-cubes v=1 format=teach grade=0 unit=9 subject=maths levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Measuring with cubes and hands"
  goal "Measure how long or tall something is in cubes or hands."

  look {
    scene 14x12 {
      towers t heights=[5, 3] labels=false at=canvas(1, 1)
    }
    say """
        This tower is five cubes tall and that one is three. Cubes in a line with no gaps measure how
        tall or long something is, and so do hands put one after another. The taller tower is two
        cubes taller.
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked k.tower-cubes h=3 level=medium
    }
    practice k.tower-cubes count=3 seed=2 easy-count=2
    practice k.taller-by count=2 seed=3
    practice k.hands-long count=2 seed=4 easy-count=1
    show k.measure-a-thing
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show k.why-no-gaps
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show k.equal-towers a=6 b=2 easy-a=5 easy-b=3 hard-a=8 hard-b=4
  }
  remember {
    say "Measure with no gaps, and count the cubes or hands."
  }

  grown-ups """
    Read each question aloud. More, fewer and the same compared counts; this lesson is the step on
    from it, comparing lengths with a unit. Building towers is the lesson: use real bricks. The
    measuring with hands is for you to watch for gaps. If the child is stuck on bigger hands giving
    a smaller number, measure the same table with your hand and theirs. Off the page, measure the
    sofa in footsteps. Japan, 幼稚園教育要領 (kindergarten guidelines), 環境, 内容 (8):
    "日常生活の中で数量や図形などに関心をもつ。" (In daily life, take an interest in numbers, amounts and
    shapes.)
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level a tower of three is counted in the teacher's pen first, the towers are short and one cube apart, and the towers to even up differ by two."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the towers are seven to nine cubes, three or four apart, the hands questions turn round, and the towers to even up differ by four."
  }
}
